# Exceptionalism in Digital Humanities

**Authors:** Michelle Moravec

## Abstract
<jats:p>In 2013 Diane Jakacki wondered “Is There Such a Thing as Digi-tal Exceptionalism” and concluded that digital humanists have “spent so long being on the outside, marginalized, trying to con-vince anyone who would listen that what we do matter and that it  is  meaningful  scholarship;  now  people  are  paying  attention.  [...] We can’t quite shake that need to justify, to foreground, to compare,  to  privilege.”1  Digital  exceptionalism  is  suffused  with  techno-optimism around what the digital can do and with the belief that the digital represents a marked, and presumably bet-ter, break with all that came before. Paul Fyfe has described the “hack versus yack” debate as digital humanities’ particular vari-ant on this theme.</jats:p>

**Source:** https://doi.org/10.21983/p3.0230.1.12

